\fs28 Methods/Functions\par
\b0\fs22 A function is a block of code that performs a specific task. \par
\par
Why are functions used? \par
---------------------------\par
If some functionality is performed at multiple places in software, then  rather than writing the same code, again and again, we create a function  and call it everywhere. This helps reduce code redundancy. \par
Functions make maintenance of code easy as we have to change at one  place if we make future changes to the functionality. \par
Functions make the code more readable and easy to understand. \par
\par
\b The syntax for function declaration is :\par
\b0 return-type function_name (parameter 1, parameter2,  \'85\'85 parameter n)\par
\{ \par
 //function_body \par
\} \par
return-type  \par
\par
The return type of a function is the data type of the variable that that function  returns.  \par
\par
For eg - If we write a function that adds 2 integers and returns their sum then  the return type of this function will be \f2\lquote\f0 int\f2\rquote\f0  as we will return a sum that is an  integer value. \par
When a function does not return any value, in that case the return type of the  function is \f2\lquote\f0 void\f2\rquote\f0 . \par
\par
function_name \par
It is the unique name of that function.  \par
It is always recommended to declare a function before it is used. \par
\par
Parameters  \par
A function can take some parameters as inputs. These parameters are specified  along with their data types. \par
For eg- if we are writing a function to add 2 integers, the parameters would be  passed like \f2\endash\f0  \par
int add (int num1, int num2)\par
\par
main function  \par
The main function is a special function as the computer starts running the code  from the beginning of the main function. Main function serves as the entry  point for the program.\par
\par
Example : \par
\par
package com.apnacollege;\par
\par
public class Main \{\par
   //A METHOD to calculate sum of 2 numbers - a & b\par
   public static void sum(int a, int b) \{\par
       int sum = a + b;\par
       System.out.println(sum);\par
   \}\par
  \par
   public static void main(String[] args) \{\par
      int a = 10;\par
      int b = 20;\par
       sum(a, b); // Function Call\par
\par
   \}\par
\}\par
