Time complexity of an algorithm quantifies the amount of time taken by an algorithm to run as a function of the length of the input. \par
Types of notations \par
1. O-notation: It is used to denote asymptotic upper bound. For a given function g(n), we denote it by O(g(n)). Pronounced as \f2\ldblquote\f0 big-oh of g of n\f2\rdblquote\f0 . It is also known as worst case time complexity as it denotes the upper bound in which the algorithm terminates. \par
2. \f1\u8486?\f0 -notation: It is used to denote asymptotic lower bound. For a given function g(n), we denote it by \f1\u8486?\f0 (g(n)). Pronounced as \f2\ldblquote\f0 big-omega of g of n\f2\rdblquote\f0 . It is also known as best case time complexity as it denotes the lower bound in which the algorithm terminates. \par
3. \b\f2\u-10187?\u-8529?\b0\f0 -notation: It is used to denote the average time of a program. \par
