\fs28 Arrays In Jav\lang1033 a\b0\fs22\par
An array in Java is a collection of elements of the same data type, stored in a continuous memory location.\par
It allows you to store multiple values in a single variable instead of declaring separate variables for each valueCreating an Array (method 1) - with new keyword\par

\pard{\pntext\f5\'B7\tab}{\*\pn\pnlvlblt\pnf5\pnindent0{\pntxtb\'B7}}\ri-874\sl240\slmult1\qj Key Features\par
{\pntext\f5\'B7\tab}\f3\u9989?\f0  Stores fixed number of elements.\par
{\pntext\f5\'B7\tab}\f3\u9989?\f0  All elements are of the same data type (e.g., all int or all String).\par
{\pntext\f5\'B7\tab}\f3\u9989?\f0  Access elements using index (starts from 0).\par
{\pntext\f5\'B7\tab}\f3\u10060?\f0  Size cannot be changed after creation.\par
{\pntext\f5\'B7\tab}If the array size is larger than needed, extra memory is unused\par
{\pntext\f5\'B7\tab}serting an element in the middle requires shifting elements\par
{\pntext\f5\'B7\tab}Arrays are mutable in Java. --> You can change the elements (contents) of an array after it has been created. \par
{\pntext\f5\'B7\tab}A \b Jagged Array \b0 is a multi-dimensional array where each row can have a different number of columns.\par

