\fs24 OOPs (Object-Oriented Programming)\fs22\par
\b0        Java is a popular OOP language, and it revolves around four main principles:\par
\par
\b\fs24  This Keyword in Java\par
\b0\fs22 Sometimes instance variables and method parameters have the same name.\par
this helps Java distinguish between them.\par

\pard{\pntext\f5\'B7\tab}{\*\pn\pnlvlblt\pnf5\pnindent0{\pntxtb\'B7}}\ri-874\sl240\slmult1\qj  Refer to Current Class Instance Variable\b\par
{\pntext\f5\'B7\tab}\b0 Call Current Class Method\b\par
{\pntext\f5\'B7\tab}\b0 Call Current Class Constructor (Constructor Chaining)\b\par
{\pntext\f5\'B7\tab}\b0 Return Current Object\b\par

\pard\ri-874\sl240\slmult1\qj\par
\fs24 Super Keyword in Java\par
\b0 The super keyword in Java is used to refer to the parent class (superclass).It helps a child class access properties and methods of its parent class.\par
\par

\pard{\pntext\f5\'B7\tab}{\*\pn\pnlvlblt\pnf5\pnindent0{\pntxtb\'B7}}\ri-874\sl240\slmult1\qj super refers to immediate parent class only\par
{\pntext\f5\'B7\tab}Used only in child class\par
{\pntext\f5\'B7\tab}If you don\f2\rquote\f0 t write super(), Java automatically calls it\par
{\pntext\f5\'B7\tab}Cannot use super inside a static method \par

\pard\ri-874\sl240\slmult1\qj\b\fs22\par
\par
\fs24 Constructor\fs22\par
\b0 A constructor is a special method in a class that is automatically called when an object is created.\b\par

\pard{\pntext\f5\'B7\tab}{\*\pn\pnlvlblt\pnf5\pnindent0{\pntxtb\'B7}}\ri-874\sl240\slmult1\qj\b0 Constructor name = Class name (in Java/C++)\par
{\pntext\f5\'B7\tab}No return type\par
{\pntext\f5\'B7\tab}Automatically called when object is created\par
{\pntext\f5\'B7\tab}Used for initializing values\par
{\pntext\f5\'B7\tab}By default Super() is the first stetaments \par

