\par
What is a Collection in Java?\par
\b0 A Collection is an object that represents a group of elements (objects). Instead of working with individual variables, collections allow you to manage multiple objects as a single unit.\par
\par
In Java, \b Collections \b0 refer to a framework that provides a set of classes and interfaces to store, retrieve, and manipulate groups of objects efficiently.\par
\par
\cf2 Collection \f1\u8594? Interface\par
Collections \u8594? Utility class (has static methods like sort(), reverse())\par
\cf0\b\par
Subinterfaces:\par

\pard 
{\pntext\f1 1.\tab}{\*\pn\pnlvlbody\pnf1\pnindent0\pnstart1\pndec{\pntxta.}}
\fi-360\li720\ri-874\sl240\slmult1\qj\b0 List\b\line\b0 Ordered collection\b\line\b0 Allows duplicates\b\line\b0 Maintains insertion order\b\line Examples: -> \b0 ArrayList, LinkedList and Vector.\b\line\par
{\pntext\f1 2.\tab}\b0 Set\b\line\b0 No duplicate elements\b\line\b0 Not ordered (except some implementations)\b\line Examples:-> \b0 HashSet, LinkedHashSet and TreeSet.\b  \line\par
{\pntext\f1 3.\tab}\b0 Queue\b\line\b0 Follows FIFO (First-In-First-Out)\b\line\b0 Used for processing tasks\b\line Examples:-> \b0 PriorityQueue and LinkedList\b\line\par
{\pntext\f1 4.\tab}\b0 Map\b\line\b0 Stores key-value pairs\line Keys are unique\line Not ordered (except some implementations)\b\line Examples:->\b0  HashMap, TreeMap and LinkedHashMap\par

\pard\ri-874\sl240\slmult1\qj\par
\par
\b What is an Iterator?\b0\par
An Iterator is an interface in Java (java.util.Iterator) that helps you read and remove elements from a collection safely.\par
\par
\f3\u10004?\f0  Works on all Collection types\par
\f3\u10004?\f0  Does not support reverse traversal (use ListIterator for that)\par
\f3\u10004?\f0  Only one-direction (forward)\par
\f3\u10004?\f0  Safer than normal loops when deleting\line\par
